<h2>Entities</h2>
<pre><code>local Entity = require("demi.entity")

local id = Entity.find("player")           -- "player", or nil if missing
if id and Entity.exists(id) then
    Entity.set_field(id, "Sprite", "color", { 1, 0.4, 0.4, 1 })
end</code></pre>
<p><code>Entity.find</code> takes the authored entity ID and returns the live handle used by every other call, or <code>nil</code> when nothing matches. <code>Entity.set_field(id, component, field, value)</code> writes one field and leaves the rest of that component unchanged — use it for runtime tweaks instead of rebuilding the component.</p>
<pre><code>local Entity = require("demi.entity")

local ok, err = Entity.spawn("crate", {
    position = { 4, 2, 0 },    -- world position, 3 floats
    velocity = { 1, 0, 0 },    -- optional initial velocity
})
-- ok: true when creation was queued; err: nil, or a diagnostic string

local enemies = Entity.query({ tags = { "enemy" } })
print(#enemies)                -- 0 when none match
Entity.destroy_many(enemies)</code></pre>
<p><code>Entity.spawn</code> takes an entity ID plus optional <code>position</code> and <code>velocity</code> shorthand (dense 2- or 3-float arrays of finite numbers). It returns <code>true</code> when creation was queued, or <code>false</code> plus an error string; the entity appears at the next creation step, so use <code>Entity.find</code> afterwards for the handle. Prefab and <code>ttl</code> options are rejected — use <code>Prefab.instantiate</code> and <code>Timer.after</code> instead.</p>
<p><code>Entity.query</code> filters by <code>tags</code>, <code>all</code>, <code>layer</code>, and <code>include_disabled</code>, and always returns an array — empty when nothing matches. Use <code>Entity.get_config(id, component, field)</code> to read one last-configured field value; for live simulation state use the relevant system (<code>Rigidbody3D.state</code>, <code>CharacterController3D.state</code>, and so on).</p>
<p>Other helpers: <code>Entity.create</code>, <code>replace</code>, <code>clone</code>, <code>destroy</code>, <code>set_enabled</code>, <code>is_enabled</code>, <code>add_component</code>, <code>remove_component</code>, <code>has_component</code>, <code>set_parent</code>, <code>parent</code>, <code>children</code>, <code>local_position</code>, <code>world_position</code>.</p>

<h2>Transforms</h2>
<pre><code>local T = require("demi.transform3d")
local x, y, z = T.get_position("player")   -- local space, 3 scalars
T.set_position("player", x + 1, y, z)
T.add_position("player", 0, 0, -2)         -- relative move

local rx, ry, rz = T.get_rotation("player")-- Euler radians
T.set_rotation("player", 0, math.pi / 2, 0)

local sx, sy, sz = T.get_scale("player")
local fx, fy, fz = T.forward("player")     -- world-space unit vector
T.look_at("player", 10, 0, 3)              -- aims at a world point</code></pre>
<p>Each getter splits its value into separate return numbers. If the entity or component is missing, every coordinate comes back <code>nil</code> — check <code>if x then … end</code>. <code>Transform3D.forward/right/up</code> return world-space unit direction vectors. <code>look_at</code> resolves the full parent chain first, so a child aims correctly regardless of nesting.</p>
<p><code>Transform2D</code> is the same pattern with <code>get/set/add_position</code> returning <code>x, y</code> and <code>get/set_rotation</code> returning a single radian angle.</p>

<h2>Prefabs and scenes</h2>
<pre><code>local Prefab = require("demi.prefab")
local Scene = require("demi.scene")

local instance = Prefab.instantiate("prefab://enemies/grunt", {
    id = "wave_4_grunt_12",              -- unique instance ID
    position = { 4, 2, 0 },
    overrides = {
        body = { name = "Elite Grunt" }, -- merge into entity "body"
    },
    pooled = true,                       -- reuse on release
})
-- instance is the root handle; children are "wave_4_grunt_12/body", …

local placed = Prefab.placements(instance)
print(#placed)

Prefab.release(instance)                 -- or release any child handle</code></pre>
<p><code>instantiate</code> expands the prefab and returns the root entity handle. Child IDs are the instance ID joined with the prefab-local ID (<code>wave_4_grunt_12/body</code>). <code>overrides</code> merges into the expanded entities by local ID: objects merge, arrays replace, and <code>null</code> removes a field. <code>pooled = true</code> keeps the entities in a pool on <code>release</code> instead of destroying them; <code>Prefab.pooled_count(id)</code> reports how many are waiting.</p>
<pre><code>Scene.prepare("scene://game/level_two")   -- start loading
-- …
if Scene.is_prepared() then
    Scene.activate()                      -- switch at frame boundary
end
print(Scene.progress())                   -- 0.0 … 1.0
print(Scene.active())                     -- current scene URI
print(Scene.error())                      -- empty string when healthy</code></pre>
<p>Loading is two-phase: <code>prepare</code> reads and decodes without changing the live world, then <code>activate</code> swaps atomically at a frame boundary. Poll <code>progress</code> or <code>is_prepared</code> while a load screen is up — both take no arguments and describe the pending prepare. <code>Scene.load</code> is the one-step form; <code>reload</code> refreshes the current scene; <code>cancel</code> aborts a prepare; <code>unload</code> frees a prepared scene; <code>set_persistent(entity_id, true)</code> marks one entity to survive scene switches.</p>

<h2>Math helpers</h2>
<pre><code>local Vector3 = require("demi.math.vector3")
local Random = require("demi.math.random")

local length = Vector3.length({ 1, 0, 0 })               -- 1.0
local n = Vector3.normalized({ 3, 0, 0 })                -- { 1, 0, 0 }
local c = Vector3.cross({ 1, 0, 0 }, { 0, 1, 0 })        -- { 0, 0, 1 }
local l = Vector3.lerp({ 0, 0, 0 }, { 10, 0, 0 }, 0.25)  -- { 2.5, 0, 0 }

Random.seed(42)              -- deterministic sequence
print(Random.value())        -- 0.0 … 1.0
print(Random.integer(1, 6))  -- inclusive bounds
local checkpoint = Random.state()
Random.restore(checkpoint)   -- rewind the sequence</code></pre>
<p>Vector functions take and return <code>Vec2</code>/<code>Vec3</code> numeric arrays — fixed-length tables indexed from 1 (<code>v[1]</code>, <code>v[2]</code>, <code>v[3]</code>); <code>length</code> and <code>dot</code> return a single number. <code>Random.state</code> snapshots the generator as an exact state string and <code>restore</code> rewinds it. Save both the seed and a state checkpoint when you need a replay to match.</p>

<h2>Events</h2>
<pre><code>local Events = require("demi.events")

local id = Events.subscribe("physics_collision_enter", function(e)
    -- e.other_id, e.point, e.normal, …
end)
Events.emit("player_died", { x = 1, y = 2 })  -- game-defined payload
Events.unsubscribe(id)</code></pre>
<p><code>subscribe</code> returns an integer token you pass back to <code>unsubscribe</code> (which returns <code>true</code> when the token was removed). <code>emit</code> returns the number of handlers delivered. Handlers run immediately on <code>emit</code>. Engine event payloads are plain tables — the fields depend on the event (see the system pages).</p>

<h2>Editor stubs</h2>
<p>Generate LuaLS/EmmyLua stubs for autocomplete in your editor:</p>
